      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in hot climates like Phoenix is often related to a combination of factors. Extreme high temperatures cause significant thermal expansion, putting considerable tensile stress on the asphalt layer. Furthermore, if the bitumen binder is too soft or lacks proper aging characteristics, it can lose its structural integrity and become more susceptible to these thermal and load-induced stresses, leading to cracking.",
