    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into more detail about the subgrade side of things? I'm wondering if poor subgrade compaction or moisture issues in the soil could be exacerbating the stress from the thermal expansion/bitumen issues you mentioned.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, a weak subgrade is a critical factor that exacerbates surface stresses. Poor compaction in the underlying soil drastically reduces the bearing capacity, allowing localized deformations under traffic loads. Additionally, moisture variations in the subgrade can lead to swelling or shrinkage, creating differential support beneath the pavement layers. These subgrade movements essentially induce stresses into the asphalt layer, making it much more vulnerable to premature cracking from thermal expansion or binder limitations.",
      "source": "target",
